WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.394
Here is a table of the class
pets. One row for each child.

2
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:12.793
A database can sort this table,
and answer a question from it.

3
00:00:13.293 --> 00:00:17.287
A database table has three
parts. Meet them one at a time.

4
00:00:17.507 --> 00:00:21.222
A record is one row. It holds
everything about one thing.

5
00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:26.783
This row is Amal's record: cat,
9 years old, 134 centimetres.

6
00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:31.322
A field is one column. It holds
one kind of fact about everybody.

7
00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:35.396
The pet field holds cat, dog,
fish, rabbit, budgie and hamster.

8
00:00:35.616 --> 00:00:40.957
Data is one cell: one fact about
one thing. Amal's pet is cat.

9
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:45.775
Record goes across. Field goes
down. Those two are easy to mix up.

10
00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:51.940
Every field has a data type. It says
what kind of thing that field holds.

11
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.597
The pet field holds words,
so its type is text.

12
00:00:55.817 --> 00:00:59.764
Age and height are counts and
measures, so their type is number.

13
00:00:59.984 --> 00:01:04.860
The day a pet was born is a date.
What its food costs is currency.

14
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.328
Store an age as text and the database
cannot sort or add it properly.

15
00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:14.683
Sorting by a field puts every
record in that field's order.

16
00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:18.479
Ascending goes up:
smallest first, or A first.

17
00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:22.692
Descending goes down:
biggest first, or Z first.

18
00:01:22.912 --> 00:01:29.089
Sort by height, descending. Zara is
tallest at 141, so she goes on top.

19
00:01:29.309 --> 00:01:35.160
Sort by pet, ascending. That is
alphabetical, so budgie comes before cat.

20
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.117
It is easy to sort the right field
the wrong way. Read which way it asks.

21
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.636
A database answers a
question when you filter it.

22
00:01:43.856 --> 00:01:48.407
Who is older than 8? Field
age, test more than, value 8.

23
00:01:48.627 --> 00:01:52.249
Only the matching records
stay. The others are hidden.

24
00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:57.345
Amal is 9, Zara is 10, Leo
is 9. Three records match.

25
00:01:57.565 --> 00:02:04.717
Now ask who has a rabbit. Field
pet, is, rabbit. One record: Omar.

26
00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:08.745
One question, one filter, one
answer, straight from the data.

27
00:02:09.245 --> 00:02:14.261
Sorting rearranges whole records,
so each child keeps their own pet.

28
00:02:14.481 --> 00:02:18.800
Filtering hides the records that
do not match. It deletes nothing.

29
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.546
Neither one changes what is inside
a cell. Amal's pet is still cat.

30
00:02:25.046 --> 00:02:29.829
A record is a row, a field is
a column, and data is one cell.

31
00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:34.925
Every field needs the right type:
text, number, date or currency.

32
00:02:35.145 --> 00:02:39.464
Sort to put records in order.
Filter to answer one question.
